{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"**3768 IMPLEMENTED AND PUSHED — Gerrit change 1169** (commit 44d9250ea), awaiting votes. Plan PASSED round 4 (0 blocking) after R1 BLOCK/3, R2 BLOCK/2, R3 BLOCK/5; signed, certified, claimed.\n\nTHE CHANGE: code-review.yaml declared a model on exactly ONE of its five LLM-calling steps. Now base->frontier (:74), round_a->batch.model_ladder [frontier] (:89), round_b->same (:127), verify->standard (:171, from 172e), coach->standard (:203). Gate re-validated CLEAN via schema.validate_document. 9 new tests; 528 passing across the verifier/model_classes/workflow suites with no regressions; lint clean; mypy clean on 407 files.\n\nMUTATION CHECK 6/6 BITE — but the FIRST attempt lied to me, and that is the lesson worth keeping. My initial M2 reported 'DID NOT BITE', which I was about to write up as a coverage gap. It was neither a gap nor a mispairing: **the mutation never applied.** My `mut()` helper did `orig.replace(old,new,1)` without checking `old` was present, and my anchor ('    model: standard\\n\\n  - id: verdict') did not exist — there are TWO `model: standard` lines (:171 verify, :203 coach) and neither is followed immediately by `- id: verdict`. So `.replace` was a silent no-op, the suite ran against UNMUTATED source, and 'PASSED' looked like a blind spot in my tests. **A mutation test that cannot fail is indistinguishable from a test suite that cannot detect — assert the anchor exists AND that the mutation actually changes the text.** Re-run with `assert old in orig` plus a no-op guard: M1 (move the batch class to step level = the silent no-op) BITES, M2b (coach->frontier) BITES, M2c (verify->frontier, the one that genuinely targets the downgrade oracle) BITES, M3 (literal model id instead of a class) BITES, M4 (lengthen the ladder to three rungs) BITES, M5 (base->standard) BITES. Source restored byte-identical; __pycache__ cleared before trusting the green.\n\nCOMMIT-MESSAGE GATE, new to me this firing: a pre-commit hook enforces the 50/72 rule and REJECTED two attempts — first the body wrapped at ~78 (limit 72), then a 53-char subject (limit 50). It reports as 'commit message (50/72 wrap; never mutates)' once passing. Note d23e's commit (subject 64 chars) landed earlier WITHOUT this rejection and Gerrit only warned on push, so this hook is either newly installed or newly reaching this worktree — budget for it on every future commit. Nothing landed on either rejection (verified with git log before retrying).\n\nREMAINING: 1169 votes -> close 3768 -> unblocks 0d76 (S6) -> eb6e (S8) -> 8625 (S9) -> 5e75 -> epic 061c -> LAND. Reuse the two-provider recipe on eb58 for 0d76's parity work, and carry the Bedrock inference-profile finding (`us.` prefix required on-demand) into 0d76/8625."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785602325895974001,"uuid":"3d6cb44e-72c7-448c-b5a5-6ffebbb8217f"}